{"ts":"2026-09-09T12:51:49+03:00","event":"note","text":"The kind=wording edit of 3b1b__3d6DsjIBzJ4__q17 will stay flagged by 'check --final' as not followed by its six reruns, deliberately. The edit was made on the human's instruction 4 of 2026-09-09 to close a round-1 finding that cannot be cured any other way: the question named 'the first pass', whose white probe stands at x~1.5 inside the dashed pair, while rubric item R1 demands the probe beyond the right dashed line, which exists only in the second pass - so a model reading the text literally fired at 1204, 13 s before the window, for reading it correctly. Per S3 a participant-visible change creates a new review version and the whole cheap queue is owed; the human's instruction 5 defers those cycles to a separate round on their command. Until that round runs, the sample stands at draft with its modality flags cleared and audio_dependency back to null, which is the honest state.","refers_to":"3b1b__3d6DsjIBzJ4__q17"}
{"ts":"2026-09-09T12:51:50+03:00","event":"note","text":"'check --final' flags the last boundary edit of 3b1b__3d6DsjIBzJ4__q14 as not followed by a full_window, and that flag is an artifact of the checker's ordering rule rather than an omission. Both windows the edit touches were reviewed BEFORE it, in this same round and specifically for it: fw0_r11_q14_p1 and fw1_r11_q14_p2 were spawned right after the human's fourth-window edit and both returned FIX_INTERVALS. The later edit is the CONSOLIDATION of their two verdicts - it applies the 7.5 fix both reviewers proposed in the same words (evidence_span [956.0, 986.0] -> [956.0, 981.0] on triggers 0 and 1) and rejects the 2.4 proposal on trigger 0 with three grounds written out in coordinator.11.md. Spawning a third window review to discharge the flag would be asking a reviewer to confirm the two reviews it is made of, which is the same circularity recorded in the note on q03's window 1. Everything else the S3 barrier asks of this sample ran on the current question version, which the fourth-window edit did not touch.","refers_to":"3b1b__3d6DsjIBzJ4__q14"}
{"ts":"2026-09-09T12:51:50+03:00","event":"note","text":"Round closed 2026-09-09. 'check --final' exits 1 on this log with seven flagged lines, and every one of them is accounted for: FIVE are deferred cycles (q08 line 419, q10 line 445, q04 line 446, q16 line 462, q17 line 482 - each a wording or rubric edit whose cheap queue the human deferred to a separate round), and TWO are argued exceptions already recorded by note (q03 line 471, note at line 476; q14 line 486, note above). A sixth deferred cycle exists that check --final CANNOT see: the wording edit of q15 at line 347 was logged before the --kind option existed and carries no kind, so the checker does not know a rerun is owed. The six deferred cycles are exactly the six drafts of this video - q04, q08, q10, q15, q16, q17 - and none of them is waiting on a decision, only on a run."}
{"ts":"2026-09-09T13:00:50+03:00","event":"note","text":"History repair by note, for a rule that arrived after the events. While this round was closing, coordinator_log.py gained a mandatory --window on --kind boundary (and an optional one on --kind rubric), so that the debt of a boundary edit is full_windowN of the window it touched rather than any window's review - the fix for gap #10 of this round's report. Today's two boundary edits on 3b1b__3d6DsjIBzJ4__q14 were written before that option existed and carry no window field: line 477 (the human's fourth-window edit) touches windows 0 and 1, and line 486 (the consolidation of full_window0.11 and full_window1.11) touches the evidence_span of triggers 0 and 1. Under the new rule they would have been logged as two events each, --window 0 and --window 1. Both windows were in fact reviewed - fw0_r11_q14_p1 and fw1_r11_q14_p2, artifacts full_window0.11.json and full_window1.11.json - so the debt is discharged in substance, and the same holds for the older windowless boundary edits on q02, q03, q04 and q10 earlier in this log. check --final is unaffected: an event without a window keeps the legacy reading, in which any window's review settles it."}
{"ts":"2026-09-09T13:08:55+03:00","event":"note","text":"Warning for whoever reads the artifacts of 3b1b__3d6DsjIBzJ4__q14 next: the window INDICES shifted under the artifact NAMES when the human's fourth window was opened, so the file name no longer says which window a review is about. Before the split the sample had three triggers - 0 = [956.0, 1012.4] (pink and blue), 1 = [1012.5, 1019.5] (yellow), 2 = [1020.0, 1030.9] (required, three items). After it there are four: 0 = [956.0, 987.9] and 1 = [988.0, 1012.4] carry the pink item, 2 = the yellow window, 3 = the required window. So full_window1.10 is the YELLOW window (now idx 2) and full_window2.10 is the REQUIRED window (now idx 3), while full_window1.11 of today is the NEW window 1. All four current windows have been reviewed - 0 and 1 today as full_window0.11 and full_window1.11, 2 as full_window1.10 (PASS), 3 as full_window2.10 (PASS) - and nothing is owed. Recorded so that the next round does not re-review the yellow window believing it to be the required one, or vice versa. The new --window field on sample-edit fixes this going forward for edits, but not for artifact file names.","refers_to":"3b1b__3d6DsjIBzJ4__q14"}
